South African doctors: Omikron transmission is strong, but mostly mild
Many doctors and medical experts in South Africa believe that the disease caused by the Omikron variant of the new coronavirus is milder than the Delta variant, although it spreads more rapidly.
According to the Associated Press, with the spread of the Omikron variant in South Africa, a group of more than 5,000 doctors in Gauteng, South Africa, reported that the symptoms of the new crown patients they are currently treating are relatively mild, such as symptoms such as physical pain and cough similar to the flu.
Dr Unben Pillay, the group’s director, said he sees dozens of patients every day, but none of them need to be hospitalized.
"They're able to control the disease at home, and most people recover within 10 to 14 days of isolation," Pillay said.
He said this included older patients and those with health problems, who would have been more likely to fall into severe illness due to coronavirus infection.
In contrast, he said, in the last wave of the Delta outbreak, he treated new crown patients with “difficult breathing and low blood oxygen levels.”
Many people will be hospitalized in a few days.”
Within two weeks of southern Africa’s first reporting of the Omikjon variant strain, other doctors shared similar experiences.
While it’s still a few weeks before sufficient data is collected, the doctor’s observations and early evidence provide some clues.
According to South Africa's National Infectious Disease Research Institute, only 30% of new crown patients hospitalized in recent weeks are serious diseases, which is half the proportion of severe diseases in the previous wave; the average length of hospitalization has been reduced from 8 days to 2.8 days; only 3% of new crown patients have died recently, compared with 20 per cent in the cases of early outbreaks in South Africa.
Willem Hanekom, director of the African Institute of Health, cited data from the National Infectious Diseases Institute and other reports: â€œAt present, almost all indications are that this is a milder disease.â€
“It’s still early days and we’re going to need later data.
Typically, hospitalizations and deaths occur late, and we enter this outbreak for only two weeks."
Meanwhile, scientists around the world are looking at the number of cases and hospitalizations while testing the effects of current vaccines and treatments.
Although Delta is still the world's leading strain of the new coronavirus, Omikron cases are emerging in dozens of countries, of which South Africa is the epicentre of the outbreak.
According to health officials, the number of new cases detected in Gauteng province in the first week of December increased by 400 percent, and tests showed more than 90 per cent of cases of the Omikron variant.
