The International Olive Oil Association divides olive oil into five categories: Virgin Olive Oil and Refined Olive Oil (Lampante Olive Oil or Refined Olive Oil).
Virgin olive oil, called natural olive oil, is oil obtained directly from the fresh olive fruit after mechanical cold pressing, filtration and other treatment to remove foreign objects.
It can be divided into three levels depending on acidity:
Extra Virgin is the highest and highest quality olive oil and is an all-natural product.
The taste is excellent, there are light and pleasant plants fragrant, the acidity is not more than 1%.
High-quality virgin olive oil (Fine Virgin): slightly higher acidity, but not more than 2%, the taste is pure and aromatic.
Ordinary Virgin (Ordinary Virgin): taste and flavor, the acidity is not more than 3.3%.
Refined olive oil refers to the olive oil obtained after refining virgin olive oil with an acidity of more than 3.3 percent, or becomes “secondary oil”.
Refined olive oil can be divided into two levels:
Olive Oil: Refined olive oil is mixed with a certain proportion of virgin olive oil to reconcillate the taste and color, and its acidity is less than 1.5%, which is transparent pale golden yellow.
Refined Olive-Pomace Oil (Refined Olive-Pomace Oil): Olive oil that is extracted from the slag by dissolution and refined.
You should have found that the acidity of olive oil is a very important indicator.
You know, olives are like grapes made of wine, and olives from different regions and different olive varieties will produce different standards of olive oil.
The best growing ground for olive oil:
The Mediterranean coast has climatic conditions such as the most suitable soil and temperature, sunshine, air humidity and precipitation required for the growth of olive oil.
With the exception of the Mediterranean coast, other areas can only be planted sporadically in suitable areas.
Main production areas (Mediterranean):
Italy, Spain, Greece, Turkey, Portugal, Morocco, Syria, Israel, Tunisia, Algeria, Egypt, Pakistan, Lebanon, etc., similar to its climate is also far away in the southern latitudes of Argentina, Peru, South Africa and so on.
Months of olive oil picking:
From March to June of each year, it is the time when the olive tree blooms, and from July to October, the fruit begins to develop and mature, and the fruit transitions from green to red, purple, and finally dark.
From November to February of the following year is the fruit picking season (the freshness of olive oil can be very important.
Nutrients of olive oil:
Olive oil is rich in monounsaturated fatty acids, i.e. oleic acid and linoleic acid, as well as vitamin A, vitamin B, vitamin D, vitamin E, vitamin K and antioxidants.
Olive oil is considered to be the most suitable oil for human nutrition in the oil found so far.
At the end of the democracy summit, Biden said that the fire of freedom will never be extinguished.
The online Summit for Democracy, convened by U.S. President Joe Biden and attended by governments from 110 countries around the world, as well as leaders of civil society and the private sector, ended Friday.
In his closing speech, Biden said that the fire of freedom burns in the hearts of everyone in the world, and authoritarian regimes will never be able to extinguish them.
He also said that he plans to convene the second global "democracy summit" next year.
A representative of the Taiwan government, government commissioner Tang Feng and Hong Kong democracy and human rights activist Luo Guancong, who is currently in exile in the United Kingdom, also spoke at the summit the next day.
Biden said: "We know how tough the work that's before us.
But we also know that we can rise to the challenge because, as I have said before, as this summit has shown, the democratic world is everywhere.
Autocratic regimes will never be able to extinguish the micro-fire of freedom, and it is burning in the hearts of people all over the world and around the world.”
Biden also said that defending democracy requires the joint efforts of society as a whole.
“As leaders of government, it is our responsibility to listen to our citizens, to strengthen the guardrails of democracy, to push for reforms, to achieve transparent and accountable governance, to be more resilient to the buffers and shocks of autocracy, and to those who want to pursue power nakedly before the public good,” he said.
The second day of the “Democracy Summit” will focus on defending human rights, consolidating democracy and countering authoritarianism, as well as countering digital authoritarianism and affirming democratic values.
This day coincides with International Human Rights Day.
In his opening speech on the same day, UN Secretary-General António Guterres also said that the cause of democracy faces global challenges, especially those brought about by emerging digital technologies.
“It’s time to reaffirm shared values and positions and democratic resilience,” he said.
This requires deepening social dialogue, addressing inequality, fighting corruption, renewing the social contract, investing in quality education, and expanding opportunities for women and girls.
It is also necessary to firmly condemn all forms of populism, nativism, and extremism, including the use of all the tools provided by the technological revolution.”
Hong Kong democracy and human rights activist Luo Guancong spoke to the summit through a pre-recorded video, reminding participants to continue to pay attention to the human rights situation of China's ethnic minorities such as Hong Kong and Uighurs and Tibetans.
"For a long time, the world has accepted China's rise, but it has not established a mechanism to hold it accountable," he said.
That’s why politicians and civil society leaders must work together.
We must use everything we have to ensure that the revival of democracy is our top priority.”
Luo also called on the free world to unite against the CCP’s threats.
"Maybe some of you are afraid to annoy General Secretary Xi Jinping, maybe some of you don't want to lose the Chinese market, and maybe some of you don't recognize the threat to our democratic values," he said.
And that’s why we failed.
That’s why we have to change.”
At the roundtable discussion of "Combating Digital Dictatorship and Affirming Democratic Values", Taiwan's executive councilor Tang Feng released the "National Statement" by video, emphasizing that Taiwan is at the forefront of the fight against autocracy and authority.
He also shared how to combat the new crown epidemic through information transparency and building mutual trust.
"As Taiwan's democracy rises, we have always stood firmly at the forefront of the world's fight against authoritarianism, and played a leading role in promoting global freedom, democracy and human rights," he said.
During the epidemic period, authoritarian governments in various places, in the name of public health and collective interests, rationalized human rights violations, and global democracy showed signs of regression.
However, in Taiwan, we don't have to lock down the city to control the epidemic, and we don't have to beat the fake information without going down."
At the same time as the "democracy summit" was scheduled for the next day, the U.S. Treasury Department announced sanctions against 15 individuals and 10 entities in several countries, such as China and North Korea, which have been identified as serious violations of human rights, while imposing investment restrictions on a Chinese company founded to have used surveillance technology to assist with human rights violations.
