Vietnam People's Daily - Gulf Cooperation Council to tackle common challenges
Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman's stops include Oman, the United Arab Emirates (UAE), Qatar, Bahrain and Kuwait.
It is worth noting that this is the first visit to Qatar by Crown Prince Mohammed since Saudi Arabia and its Arab allies imposed an embargo on Qatar in mid-2017, leading to a diplomatic crisis in the Gulf.
During the crisis, Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Bahrain, and Egypt severed diplomatic ties with Qatar and imposed a comprehensive embargo, accusing it of supporting terrorist groups while having close ties with Iran.
Diplomatic tensions have had a serious impact on the internal unity of the GCC and had a negative impact on the economies of the countries of the region.
Four Arab countries have blocked land, sea and air routes to Qatar.
The crisis was resolved only at the Gulf Cooperation Council summit in January 2021, with Gulf states signing an agreement mediated by the U.S. and Kuwait towards “unity and stability.”
Saudi Arabia has reopened its borders with Qatar.
Saudi Arabia and Egypt are spearheading efforts to restore relations with Qatar through the appointment of new ambassadors in Qatar.
The Arab states of the Gulf resolved their differences in order to restore relations with Qatar stemmed from the need to unite in the interests of all countries and regional stability, with the aim of addressing common challenges.
During the visit of the Saudi Crown Prince to Oman, the two sides agreed to open the first direct land route between the two neighboring countries in the Gulf.
The 725-kilometer-long route connecting the two countries will facilitate the travel and integration of supply chains for citizens of the two countries.
Saudi Arabia’s Public Investment Fund (PIF) will invest $5 billion in Oman, and companies from the two countries have signed 13 memorandums of understanding on cooperation worth $30 billion.
Saudi Arabia's crown prince's visit to the Gulf states coincided with Iran's talks with world powers to salvage an Iranian nuclear deal known as the JCPOA, an agreement condemned by Gulf Arab countries for believing they could not resolve the missile issue with Iran.
Gulf states have always viewed Iran as a long-term “competitor” and Iran’s nuclear program as a “threat” to regional security.
The Gulf states argue that not addressing Iran’s missile problems and not taking action to curb Iran’s activities in the region is flawed.
In the context of the failure of major powers to meet the aspirations of various Gulf states, Saudi Arabia has held direct negotiations with Iran.
The goal is to contain the escalation of tensions in the region.
This is one of the factors that has slowed the economic growth of the GCC countries.
The upcoming GCC summit will be the first since the end of a dispute between some member states and Qatar.
This is an opportunity for the Gulf Cooperation Council to reassess its diplomatic contribution to the resumption of GCC reunification, while proposing measures to enable its member States to cooperate fully to meet common challenges and contribute to the stability and development of the Gulf States.
