Asian-American group complains about discrimination in admissions of three Ivy League schools such as Yale - Xinhua
WASHINGTON, May 23 (Xinhua) -- More than 130 Asian-American groups in the United States filed complaints with the U.S. Department of Education and the Department of Justice on May 23 to protest against three prominent Ivy League schools - Yale University, Brown University and Dartmouth College discriminating against Asian-American applicants during the admissions process.
The Asian-American Education Alliance, which led the appeal, held a press conference in Washington on the same day.
The group's chairman, Zhao Yukong, said at the press conference that in the past 10 years, the number of Asian applicants who meet the admission conditions of famous schools in the United States has increased exponentially, but among these three famous U.S. schools, the proportion of Asians has basically remained at the same level.
Many studies have shown that these colleges and universities, in the so-called "comprehensive consideration" of the admissions process, "long-term continuity and systematic discrimination against Asian-American applicants."
According to a 2014 study by scholars such as Saunders, among these three colleges and universities, Asians have the lowest acceptance rate among all ethnic groups compared to the number of applicants.
The U.S. Department of Education data also shows that Brown and Dartmouth College have the lowest rates of acceptance for Asian-American students in all Ivy League schools, and Yale University and Brown University have implemented de facto racial quotas for Asian-American students for more than 20 years.
Zhao told Xinhua that after submitting the complaint, the U.S. Ministry of Education and the Ministry of Justice will be able to launch an investigation and take measures to prevent these colleges and universities from continuing to discriminate against Asians in their admissions.
They also expect colleges and universities to take the initiative to pay attention to this issue and change their enrollment policies for Asians.
At present, the three universities that have complained have not yet responded.
The Asian Education Alliance was founded in 2015 when it complained of discrimination in admissions at Harvard University, but the U.S. Department of Education did not accept it on procedural grounds.
In addition to Chinese-American groups, they also included U.S.-Indian, Korean, Japanese and Pakistani education groups.
