Sabah 3,200 newborns without remunerative paper due to special anti-counterfeiting paper outage
Sabah's National Registration Bureau said that due to the use of special anti-counterfeiting paper inventory, it has not been able to issue newspaper paper for newborn babies since the beginning of October, and 3,200 people have been affected so far.
However, the director of the bureau, Kailu Aminus, assured that all infants who have applied for newspaper paper have been recorded in the system, and when anti-counterfeiting paper is shipped next week, it will be replenished.
At the press conference, he gave an explanation in response to a 10-month report on the issue of Sabah newspaper printing.
He said that the relevant problems appeared two months ago, and Sarawak is facing the same problem.
"One of the reasons is the production of special anti-counterfeiting paper Chinese manufacturers affected by the epidemic, resulting in delayed arrival in China."
He explained that the anti-counterfeiting paper used in Sabah newspaper paper is different from that used in other areas, so it is not possible to borrow inventory from other states.
Kailu Aminus revealed that the latest batch of anti-counterfeiting paper has arrived in Kuala Lumpur on the 6th of this month, and then handed over to the printing company on the 8th to carry out partial printing work.
"The bureau will send someone to Kuala Lumpur next Thursday to pick it up, back to Sabah for the final stage of printing, it is estimated that more than 2 working days (December 20) can be handed over to the applicant."
â€œWe will inform the applicant to go to the local bureaux to collect newspaper paper.â€
He added that Sabah has issued a total of 24,801 newspaper papers so far this year.
