Don't frighten people on the starting line - Xinhua
When visiting the book market, you can often feel the popularity of successful learning books.
Many of today's successful books preach the â€œbest principleâ€ â€“ i.e., you can â€œdo itâ€ , and then tell you what you need to do â€œwhatâ€ .
It is equivalent to saying that it has suddenly crossed the most important and fundamental link of "how to do it".
Because of this, these books have one thing in common â€“ they either do their best, or they simply don't do anything; they're 100 or they're 0; or you've climbed Mount Tai, or you're just staying there.
This â€œbest principleâ€ , so that everyone will admit that â€œrightâ€ , but 99% of people will be intimidated in front of the starting line.
Because this theory preaches a path of "you have to make up your mind, to be human again, to make a 180-degree great transformation", which is precisely the fatal weakness of many people.
For those who abide by the rules, adhere to the old age, and refuse to change, you just have to shout "you have to change" in his ears, which is undoubtedly self-defeating and playing the piano.
The vast majority of people will be timid in front of the word "transformation", wrapped up, difficult to resolve; or with the impulse to pain the determination of "transformation", and bravely took the first step, but often the good situation is not long, can't stick to it for a little while to lose, it is difficult to see continuity.
Mr. Ma Sanli's famous "starting from tomorrow" is the most vivid expression of this "universal humanity" classic.
The biggest problem with the "best principle" is that the threshold is too high, and it often exceeds the limits of ordinary people's capabilities, so that the vast majority of people "look and sigh".
It may be that the "second good principle" is a way to put more ordinary people on the road to relative success.
The core of the â€œquiescent principleâ€ is to â€œlow down the thresholdâ€ â€“ it encourages a theory of â€œbuilding a small victory for a big victoryâ€ , that is, if you can't do 100, you can only do 1: because 1 will always be greater than 0; if you can't climb Taishan, you can only climb the little sand dunes on your doorstep, because that's always stronger than being in place.
In short, as long as you do something, it’s always better than not.
Because the threshold is greatly reduced, there are more possibilities for ordinary people to persist, and the probability of success will be increased as long as everything persists.
It is only the road to success that follows the "quicked principle", and its path is slightly longer.
In short, although the â€œbest principleâ€ is on the surface under the banner of making everyone successful, but it can eventually inspire, in fact, is still a minority, and the â€œsecondary principleâ€ can effectively inspire more people and benefit as many people as possible.
