Metaphysics refers to the study of questions that cannot be answered directly through perception through rational reasoning and logic.
Metaphysics is the basic law of philosophy, designed to explain existence and the fundamental nature of the world.
The Chinese translation name "metaphysics" is taken from the phrase "the way of the metaphysical, the metaphysical", and is translated by phymetasic for the Japanese Inoui (Meiji era).
Metaphysical branches include ontology, natural theology, and its extension problems.
Ontology is "to study which nouns represent the real existence of entities, and which nouns only represent a concept."
Metaphysical naturalism is the denial of God’s existence.
Its theoretical basis holds that all phenomena can be explained by the concept of natural justification, proposed by the philosophers who supported science and evolution.
Methodal naturalism refers to the study of the word God.
Spiritual philosophy is a branch of philosophy that studies the nature of consciousness and their relationship to the body (especially the brain).
Dualism and monism are the most important two schools of thinking about “the relationship between the mind and the body.”
Knowledge is a branch of philosophy that explores the nature, origin, and scope of knowledge.
Ethics is the discipline of systematic thinking and study of the moral life of humanity; in this case, morality is defined as all the codes of conduct recognized by a group of people or a culture.
It is mainly divided into metaethics (an abstract study of the nature of ethical theory or criticism), normative ethics (judging different moral views, and giving moral guidelines for correct or wrong behavior.
It studies what kind of ethical conduct people should follow, applied ethics (academies that apply ethical theories to actual living conditions, including bioethics, political ethics, etc.), descriptive ethics (collecting information about how people live and summarizing them from the observed patterns).
Its examination of ethics does not start from the preconceived theory, but investigates the real choices made and observed by actual moral agents, which contribute to all the moral factors of this choice, which examines the ethics held by social groups.
The history of ethics includes Greco-Roman ethics and medieval Christian ethics.
The first part: the ethical ideas of humanism, represented by Vara and Montaigne and others.
Part II: The Ethical Thought of the Reformation, represented by Calvin.
The third part: the ethics of politics, represented by Macchiavelli and others, modern ethics (17th-18th century, British empiricist ethics, representatives: Hume, etc., 17th-18th centuries, continental rationalist ethics, representatives: Spinoza, etc., 18th century, French Enlightenment ethics, representatives: Voltaire, etc., 18th and 19th century, German academic ethics, representatives: Kant and Hegel, etc.
Logic is the study of “the principles and standards of valid inferences and proofs.”
Logic usually consists of three parts: inductive reasoning, retrospective reasoning, and deductive reasoning, which can be divided into formal logic and non-formal logic.
Aesthetics is a subject based on the study of the nature of beauty and its significance.
