MR. RICHARD: To my friends.
Here, I would like to express my sincere gratitude to my friends for the humanitarian assistance extended to us for more than 30 years after the death of their loved ones, in particular those that have so far not been interrupted since I issued an appeal for humanitarian assistance on behalf of my friends at home and abroad at the beginning of 1994.
At present, most of the elderly parents of the victims have thanked the world, and the orphans have also grown into family jobs.
It’s your kindness that has helped these hard families get through the most difficult moments.
At present, the new crown epidemic is raging around the world, and the majority of donors have also retired.
Therefore, I earnestly ask you to suspend the humanitarian donation to our family, the Tiananmen Mothers, which should have come to a perfect end long ago.
I will always remember your love and firmly believe that your righteousness will be remembered for history.
For various reasons, I can only express my wishes in this way, ask my friends to understand!
Please accept my sincere blessings.
Let me take this opportunity to make a brief introduction to the 32-year history of humanitarian assistance by the American Federation of Self-study (IFCSS) and the Minnesota China Democracy Foundation (MCDF).
In the process of engaging in humanitarian assistance, the All-American Self-Defense Union and the Minnesota China Democracy Foundation are two parallel organizations, and the implementers of the project often intersect.
(Apple Daily Photo: Tian'an Yin mother 親網 station by 陶業負貲責) pic.twitter.com/UxID9ZGjzm
The rescue money often does not go smoothly into the hands of the June 4 difficulty.
In 1994, Mr. Ding suggested that a special group should be set up by the "Tiananmen Mothers" to coordinate the receipt and distribution of the way's relief funds.
In 1998, MCDF set up the June 4 Orphans Education Fund, 11 years after its implementation, in 2009, after graduating from the university of all June 4 orphans, MCDF then set up the "Sixty-four orphan elderly special hardship assistance fund", which has been implemented to this day.
Funding for these projects came primarily from campus and social donations from June 4, generous donations from individuals, and supported by the Fraternal University Foundation for Democracy.
Since 2018, it has also received support from the Labor Reform Research Foundation.
Until last year, after a lapse of 31 years, the victims finally received some of the donations raised in the name of June 4 from Hong Kong.
During this period, Human Rights in China is also independently carrying out their humanitarian assistance programs.
We understand that Mr. Ding's suspension of donations is for the consideration of donors, and we are indeed some pensioners, and the income is not as abundant as before retirement, and I would like to express our gratitude for Mr. Ding's letter to his friends.
We will respect their decision to terminate the project within two years and, if necessary, to recover immediately.
Thank you and pay tribute to those who have insisted on giving generously for many years and taking the risk of delivering humanitarian relief.
