Sino-Saudi curator joins hands to hold Saudi Arabia's 1st Contemporary Art Biennale - Xinhua
RIYADH, Dec. 12 (Xinhua) -- Saudi Arabia's first Biennale of Contemporary Art, the Diriyah Contemporary Art Biennale, was officially opened to the public on December 11 in the Diriyah JAX Art District, northwest of Saudi Arabia's capital, with a total of 64 artists from Saudi Arabia, China and other places participating.
The Biennale is co-curated by the Saudi Diriyah Biennale Foundation and the UCCA Ullens Center for Contemporary Art in China, with Tian Weiyu, Director of the UCCA Ullens Center for Contemporary Art in China, as artistic director.
According to the curatorial team, the theme of the Biennale is "crossing the river with stones", which metaphorically reflects the reform strategy adopted during the period of socio-economic transformation, and also reflects the current situation of social and cultural development in Saudi Arabia.
Covering an area of nearly 12,000 square meters, the Biennale spans six interconnected buildings and is presented in six units: "Touching Stones Across the River", "Inheritance as an Experiment", "Edge Thinking", "Towards the Public", "Brave New World" and "On the Spirit of Art".
Among them, the number of works by 27 Saudi artists accounted for more than 40% of the total number of exhibits, including paintings, installation arts and performing arts and other forms; 12 Chinese artists' works have also attracted much attention, and some artists have used recycled materials to create in novel ways, such as Xu Bing's endless picture of Xu Bing's "Store Series" (2014), reconstructing a classic landscape painting with waste.
Barkley, CEO of the Diriyah Biennale Foundation, expressed the hope that the Biennale will establish an international dialogue, so that global audiences have the opportunity to learn about Saudi Arabia's flourishing cultural industries and creative communities, and strengthen interactive exchanges.
“The goal of the Biennale is twofold, both to allow Saudi contemporary art to participate in the global dialogue, but also to integrate Saudi audiences into the world of contemporary art.”
It is understood that a series of lectures will also be held during the opening week of the Biennale, and artists, curators and opinion leaders in the creative industry will discuss topics such as cross-cultural exchanges and the development of contemporary art.
