In the past two years, the blockade and containment of Huawei's chips have not only plunged Huawei into an unprecedented dilemma, but also caused huge losses to "American" enterprises.
The U.S. emperor suppressed China's chip industry, requiring companies from their own enterprises and allies to block China's chip industry at the same time, the direct consequence is that the orders and profits of global chip suppliers are affected, resulting in reduced R&D investment and reduced production capacity of chip production enterprises, resulting in the global chip supply chain shortage.
Nowadays, the "lack of core" has spread to the world's major industries, such as the shutdown of car companies, mobile phone prices, and almost all industries that use chips have suffered greatly.
In order to grasp the autonomy of "chips", more and more tech giants in various countries have begun to the path of "de-beautification".
In order to firmly control the control of global semiconductors and contain the development of China's semiconductor industry, the old and U.S. has recently taken frequent actions and presided over the "chip summit"; it has invited 64 Chinese giants to set up a semiconductor alliance, and to support domestic technology giants in the United States, for example, in order to regain the dominant position in the chip field, Intel spent $20 billion to build two wafer processing factories in Arizona, and open up chip foundry business.
When most people think that Intel's move will have a certain impact on TSMC, and it is likely to steal TSMC's "rice bowl".
The reason for this affirmation is that Intel, as a veteran technology giant of the United States, has cultivated in the field of semiconductors for decades, with a large team of talents and technical reserves, which will surely be able to rise rapidly in the short term and defeat TSMC.
However, for Intel's decision, TSMC founder Zhang Zhongmou disdainful.
At the "2021 Masters Think Tank Forum" held in April this year, Zhang Zhongmou spoke amazingly, for Intel's move to build a fab, Zhang Zhongmou had no scruples: Intel's construction of chip foundries is quite ironic.
The reason why Zhang Zhongmou is quite disdainful of Intel's entry into the field of wafers is that Zhang Zhongmou is still worried about a part of TSMC and Intel more than 30 years ago.
In the 1960s and 1970s, Intel has always been the pearl of the U.S. imperial manufacturing industry, ranking at the top of the world's top position, and is the world's leader in the field of semiconductors.
By the 1980s, the world's high-end semiconductor chips had been mastered by a small number of strong people.
At that time, the semiconductor industry was characterized by IDM mode, and the chips from design, manufacturing to sealing and testing were completed by a factory, and Intel was the representative of that era.
However, such a model makes some small businesses overwhelmed, and many semiconductor entrepreneurs can only be discouraged in the face of huge production line construction costs.
Under the inspiration of market demand, after entering the 1980s, the chip industry began to appear division of labor and cooperation phenomenon, in 1984 Zhang Zhongmou heard that there is a company specializing in chip design, it believes that since there is a special design, then you can set up a factory dedicated to wafer manufacturing.
Soon after, Zhang Zhongmou's vision became a reality, and TSMC came into being.
TSMC aimed the main business at the single link of "walking processing", did not design, and only provided foundry services for wafer manufacturing.
TSMC, which had just been founded at that time, was small in front of Intel's Big Mac.
In order to obtain capital support, Zhang Zhongmou once sought Intel investment, but at that time Intel heard that TSMC only does the manufacturing link of the chip industry, it is obvious that some self-esteem is very high, look down on TSMC, and decisively refused to invest in TSMC.
Zhang Zhongmou also determined that Intel at that time looked down on wafer process services.
I didn't expect to look down on the giants of wafer manufacturing services at that time, but now I also have to start to do wafer foundries.
Therefore, Zhang Zhongmou feels that it is ironic for Intel to enter the wafer foundry.
Today, TSMC's market value has surpassed Intel's.
In the face of TSMC's strong attack, Intel naturally did not dare to stand still.
Today, TSMC has sprinted towards a 3nm chip, and Intel is still stuck at 14nm seven years ago.
It’s actually not easy to catch up.
